TEST
1 - Pure CSS Collapsible Tree View (No JavaScript)
Construct a nested, infinite-depth folder tree UI using pure HTML (<input type="checkbox"> or <details>/<summary>) and CSS.
	Requirements:
	Expanding or collapsing a parent folder toggles only its immediate and sub-children.
	Connect tree nodes using dashed vertical and horizontal lines (::before / ::after) that automatically terminate cleanly at the last leaf node.
2- box-sizing: content-box vs. border-box: If a <div> has width: 200px, padding: 20px, and border: 5px solid black, what is its total rendered width under content-box versus border-box? Why is *, *::before, *::after { box-sizing: border-box; } standard practice in CSS resets?

3- Specificity Calculation Battle: Calculate the specificity score (Inline, ID, Class/Attribute/Pseudo-class, Element/Pseudo-element) for each of the following selectors and determine which rule wins for <button class="btn btn-primary" id="submit" type="submit">:
	Rule A: div#container button.btn.btn-primary
	Rule B: #submit[type="submit"]
	Rule C: body #submit.btn
4 - position: absolute vs. position: fixed vs. position: sticky:
	What determines the containing block (reference ancestor) for an element with position: absolute?
	What common CSS property applied to an ancestor element can accidentally break position: fixed, forcing it to behave like position: absolute?
	Why does position: sticky fail to stick if an ancestor has overflow: hidden?
5- Why can't you mix BigInt and Number in standard arithmetic operations (e.g., 10n + 5)? How do you create unique hidden object keys using Symbol, and why are they ignored by JSON.stringify()?
6- Mutating vs. Non-Mutating Methods: Group these array methods into Mutating (alters the original array) and Non-Mutating (returns a new array or value):splice(), slice(), pop(), map(), sort(), filter(), reverse(), toReversed(), fill().
7 - Arrays: Write a program to create an array of 10 numbers and print the following:
- All even numbers
- All odd numbers
- The sum of all array elements
- The total number of even and odd elements

8 - Write a function normalizeData(data) that takes an array containing mixed and nested types (NaN, null, undefined, integers, floats as strings, boolean strings, nested arrays).
	Rules:
	String numbers ("42", "3.14") → convert to native numbers.
	String booleans ("true", "false") → convert to actual booleans.
	Remove all null, undefined, and NaN values completely.
	Flatten all nested arrays by exactly 1 level.
	Use standard iteration or array methods without relying on eval() or .flat().
9 - Write a function calculateUtilityBill(units, customerType) calculating an electricity bill using tiered bracket pricing and conditions:
	Tiers (Residential):
	First 100 units: ₹3.00/unit
	Next 200 units (101–300): ₹4.50/unit
	Above 300 units: ₹6.00/unit
	Tiers (Commercial):
	Flat ₹7.00/unit for all units, but if total units exceed 500, apply a 10% surcharge on the total bill.
	Requirements:
	Refactor to avoid deeply nested if/else statements (use clean lookup arrays or early exits).
	Throw an error if units is negative or not a valid number.

10 - The document.write() Tokenizer Interruption: What happens when an inline <script> calls document.write("</div><span>") while the HTML parser is actively constructing the DOM? Under what exact network and parsing conditions will modern Chromium/WebKit browsers intervene and refuse to execute document.write()?

